  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...A brief period of dangerous heat today, with very \nwarm, but less humid conditions Wednesday.\n\nHeat Advisories remain in effect across western NY through 8 PM EDT \nas extensive high pressure system remains across the southern Great \nLakes region. The 12z Buffalo sounding recorded 850mb temp of 21 \ndegC, which is in the climatological 99th percentile for mid-July. \nObserved temperatures early this afternoon have reach the mid-80s to \naround 90 so far. Coolest areas remain downwind of the lakes near \nBuffalo and Watertown where southwest winds gusting around 30 mph \ncontinue to provide some relief. \n\nMonitoring temperatures through the morning hours and early \nafternoon, the NBM has been running 2-4 degF too warm across much of \nwestern NY, likely due to lingering upper level smoke from Canadian \nwildfires rounding the ridge. Latest GOES Geocolor imagery shows a \nclearing now overhead that will remain in place through this \nafternoon, so still expecting feels like temperatures to reach into \nthe upper 90s through late this afternoon, especially across the \nlake plains, Genesee Valley, and northern Finger Lakes region. \nHowever, satellite imagery shows additional smoke across the Upper \nGreat Lakes that will approach western NY overnight and into \nWednesday morning behind a cold front moving south out of Canada. \n\nTemperatures will remain warm Wednesday, but relief from the humid \nconditions will arrive throughout the day as the cold front \ncontinues to drop south across the region. Additionally, expecting \ncloud cover in the morning near the frontal boundary and a slight \nchance (~20%) of showers into the afternoon.\n\nKey Message 2...Showers and thunderstorms across the North Country \nthis evening and overnight. \n\nNumerous thunderstorm complexes are ongoing currently across Quebec \nriding overtop of the extensive upper level ridge in the Great Lakes \nregion. This is all within a well of 2000-3000 J/kg of CAPE \nextending from northern NY and north of the border into Canada that \nwill allow for the continuation of strong and severe storms to \ndevelop and be maintained with 50 kt of effective shear moving \nsoutheast. However, forecast soundings near Watertown and the St. \nLawrence Valley show considerable convective inhibition remaining in \nplace before the cap can potentially break late this evening.\n\nIf storms can develop tonight, strong downburst winds will be the \nmain hazard. Overall timing looks to be around 9 PM EDT for storms \nto begin approaching the St. Lawrence Valley, possibly clipping \nnorthern Jefferson and Lewis Co starting around midnight and into \nthe early morning hours. Light showers will remain along the \nfrontal boundary through Wednesday morning as it continues to \ndrop south through our area.",
